    "question": {
      ":question/id": "305338aa-6b39-5290-b15b-f13160ac8623",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ when $f(x)=(-8-2x)^{2}.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=g^{2},\\,g=-8-2x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=2g,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=-2.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} &=2\\cdot (-2)\\cdot (-8-2x) \\\\  &=-4(-8-2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
